{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"327382f9-a160-4ded-8924-256dccae4eb4","title":"Superposition of two waves: constructive and destructive"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"327382f9-a160-4ded-8924-256dccae4eb4","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Superposition means adding displacements at each position. Compare waves with amplitudes one and point six: aligned on the left, opposite on the right.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"constructive","region":"left","frame":{"x":[0,6.3],"y":[-1.9,1.9],"axes":"edge","x_label":"position","y_label":"displacement"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"wave_a","y":"sin(x)","domain":[0,6.28]},{"kind":"fn","id":"wave_b","y":"0.6*sin(x)","domain":[0,6.28],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[3.15,1.75],"text":"Constructive"}]},{"op":"figure","id":"destructive","region":"right","frame":{"x":[0,6.3],"y":[-1.9,1.9],"axes":"edge","x_label":"position","y_label":"displacement"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"wave_c","y":"sin(x)","domain":[0,6.28]},{"kind":"fn","id":"wave_d","y":"-0.6*sin(x)","domain":[0,6.28],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[3.15,1.75],"text":"Destructive"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"327382f9-a160-4ded-8924-256dccae4eb4","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Aligned crests reinforce. At this crest, one plus point six gives a resultant displacement of one point six.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"add_left","on":"constructive","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"sum_left","y":"1.6*sin(x)","domain":[0,6.28],"label":"resultant"},{"kind":"point","id":"crest_left","at":{"on":"sum_left","x":1.5707963268},"label":"1.6"},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.5707963268,0],"to":{"at":"crest_left"},"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"sum_rule","anchor":{"el":"constructive","side":"below","gap":0.04},"latex":"y=y_1+y_2=(1+0.6)\\sin x=1.6\\sin x"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"327382f9-a160-4ded-8924-256dccae4eb4","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Opposite displacements subtract, leaving amplitude point four. This is partial destructive interference; equal amplitudes in opposition would cancel completely.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"add_right","on":"destructive","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"sum_right","y":"0.4*sin(x)","domain":[0,6.28],"label":"resultant"},{"kind":"point","id":"crest_right","at":{"on":"sum_right","x":1.5707963268},"label":"0.4"},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.5707963268,0],"to":{"at":"crest_right"},"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"difference","anchor":{"el":"destructive","side":"below","gap":0.04},"latex":"y=y_1+y_2=(1-0.6)\\sin x=0.4\\sin x"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"What results when two equal-amplitude waves are exactly in opposition?","expected_gist":"Complete cancellation: zero resultant displacement."}}}
